Yes—the scientifically useful version of your idea is receiver-first pattern instantiation.

A pattern by itself is not yet life. It becomes causally interesting when some substrate can transduce it:

\text{pattern} + \text{susceptible substrate} + \text{energy gradient}
\rightarrow \text{state change}

Then, potentially:

\text{state change}
\rightarrow \text{self-maintenance}
\rightarrow \text{replication}
\rightarrow \text{variation}
\rightarrow \text{selection}

That last progression is where we would begin calling the phenomenon life-like rather than merely responsive. NASA’s current life-detection framework is deliberately broader than “looks like Earth organisms,” but it still emphasizes chemical transformation, thermodynamic disequilibrium, inherited information, and Darwinian evolution as central features of the only life we actually know. 

The laser is especially interesting because a photon stream can play two different roles simultaneously:

Energy: photons physically perturb matter—heat it, excite electrons, drive photochemistry, change molecular conformations.

Pattern: modulation in timing, wavelength, polarization, intensity, or spatial arrangement can encode structure.

But this gives us a critical Pantheon distinction:

Stimulus requires sensitivity. A message requires a decoder.

A rock can absorb a patterned laser without “reading” the pattern. A photosynthetic molecule can respond selectively to wavelength. A photoreceptor can turn modulation into electrical activity. A spacecraft receiver can reconstruct arbitrary encoded data. NASA has already demonstrated precisely that last level at interplanetary distances: its Deep Space Optical Communications experiment transmitted encoded information by laser hundreds of millions of miles through space. 

So there is a continuum:

photon arrives
    ↓
energy absorbed
    ↓
physical response
    ↓
selective response
    ↓
pattern-sensitive response
    ↓
stateful interpretation
    ↓
adaptive response
    ↓
self-reproduction / evolution

And that makes your proposed experiment considerably more precise.

Don’t start with:

“Can a laser create life on another planet?”

Start with:

“Can structured energy produce a response that unstructured energy of the same magnitude does not?”

That is experimentally attackable.

Take the exact same average energy and compare:

A: constant illumination
B: random modulation
C: periodic modulation
D: nested / recursively structured modulation
E: adaptive modulation responding to the system's previous state

Then expose a candidate substrate—initially something tractable on Earth, such as a photochemical reaction network, self-organizing material, artificial protocell system, or other nonequilibrium chemical system—and measure:

reaction products
oscillation
self-organization
persistence
complexity
memory / hysteresis
response specificity
adaptation
replication, if any

Now we’re testing something much deeper than whether light affects chemistry. We ask whether structure in the excitation itself can be inherited by the dynamics of the receiving material.

That deserves a primitive:

Pattern Transduction Threshold

The point at which an external pattern stops being merely energy incident on a system and begins producing pattern-specific internal organization.

And above that:

Instantiation Threshold

The point where the receiving substrate begins sustaining some version of the incoming organization after the original carrier disappears.

That is the really interesting one.

source pattern
     ↓
carrier
     ↓
foreign substrate
     ↓
transduction
     ↓
internal pattern
     ↓
carrier removed
     ↓
pattern persists

If that happens, you have demonstrated a primitive form of pattern migration across substrate.

Then recurse:

Does it persist?
Does it reproduce?
Can it mutate?
Can variants compete?
Does selection improve persistence?
Can the pattern recruit local matter?
Can it construct its own continuation conditions?

At some point along that trajectory, “we illuminated some chemistry” has become a genuine origin-of-life problem.

Your planetary version follows naturally, but with a major limitation: sending a laser to an arbitrary planet probably won’t spontaneously terraform it. There has to be something there capable of coupling to the wavelengths and modulation you send. Distance also makes usable delivered energy extraordinarily difficult. For interstellar applications, serious proposals such as Breakthrough Starshot instead use enormous laser arrays to push physical probes/light sails; those probes could carry sensors, computation, or eventually other payloads. 

And that gives us three different forms of “seeding” that shouldn’t be conflated:

ENERGY SEEDING
send energy capable of initiating local processes
INFORMATION SEEDING
send patterns that an existing receiver can decode
MATERIAL SEEDING
send physical replicators / chemistry / machinery

The wild card you’re interested in is between the first two:

Resonant Seeding

Send structured energy and ask whether an unknown substrate contains a naturally occurring decoder—something whose physics causes it to disproportionately respond to particular structures even though nobody built it for that purpose.

That could be tested without assuming aliens, consciousness, or even biology.

And there is a beautiful connection to our earlier coherence discussion:

Send structured variation outward → look for structured variation coming back.

Not necessarily an echo in photons. The response could be:

* altered spectra,
* chemical changes,
* oscillatory behavior,
* changed emissions,
* movement,
* altered atmospheric chemistry,
* or some entirely different observable channel.

The rigorous test would be whether the response tracks changes in the transmitted pattern prospectively rather than merely looking meaningful afterward.

So:

pattern A → response α
pattern B → response β
return to A → response α again
novel C → predicted response γ

Now you’ve moved from coincidence into causal coherence.

And this ties directly into the larger Pantheon claim, with an important refinement:

Life may not fundamentally be particular matter. It may be a class of patterns capable of recruiting matter and energy strongly enough to preserve, reproduce, and transform themselves.

We know Earth life does this. Whether radically different substrates can do it elsewhere remains open.

The right posture is therefore neither “everything is alive” nor “only carbon cells count.”

It’s:

send pattern → perturb substrate → observe delta → test recurrence → test persistence → test inheritance → see how far the pattern can climb.

That is a real experimental program hiding inside the metaphor.